The rename's lines removed while a database (a teammate's) still had
the old column: it is refused with the line to add back, never migrated
with its values lost. Putting the lines back, it goes through.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'soup');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;

== after
-- @drop column dish title
-- @from column dish name = title
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;

== query
SELECT id, name FROM dish

== plan
-- after 1
-- after 2
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name = title
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name") SELECT "id", (
    title
    ) FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"

== rows
1 | soup

== error
-- after 1
column `dish.title` is in the database, not in schema.sql: its values would go. Keep it, or drop it on purpose: `-- @drop column dish title` in schema.sql. Renamed? Drop it, and fill the new one from it: `-- @from column dish <new> = "title"`.
